    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d257">

                <pb facs="0373" n="267" sameAs="frus:frus1906p1/pg267"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to Minister
                        Rockhill.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1906-04-13"><hi rendition="#italic">April 13,
                        1906</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 131.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: On the 10th ultimo the department sent to
                    the Secretary of the Interior copies of your dispatch No. 206, of January 21
                    last, and of instructions Nos. 114 and 117, dated, respectively, the 2d and 10th
                        <pb facs="0374" n="268" xml:id="pg268"/> ultimo, and he was asked whether he
                    had any additional points to suggest that would serve as a basis for further
                    instructions to you, with the view to securing such amendments to the Chinese
                    mining regulations as would bring them more into harmony with our treaty with
                    China and render them more practical for industrial and commercial purposes.</p>
                <p>I inclose herewith, for your information and guidance, a copy of the answer of
                    the Secretary of the Interior and of the inclosures to his letter.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Bacon</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>



